Chapter: IntRem

 Codex has two remainders and they answer different questions.

 `int-rem` is the truncating remainder: it takes the sign of the
 DIVIDEND, and it is what `idiv` leaves in RDX with no correction
 applied. It is the remainder that pairs with `/`, which truncates
 toward zero, so for every pair the identity

   a == (a / b) * b + int-rem a b

 holds -- including when either operand is negative. That identity is
 the reason this builtin exists; before it, the only remainder Codex
 exposed made the identity false for every negative dividend.

 `int-mod` is Euclidean: its answer is never negative, whatever the
 signs of the operands. It does not pair with `/` and is not meant to.
 It is measured in the same rows here so that the two contracts are
 legible side by side and so a change to one that disturbed the other
 would be visible.

 Both operands arrive as parameters at every call site. A literal
 dividend folds at compile time, which would measure the constant
 folder rather than the emitter; a literal divisor takes the
 power-of-two strength reduction, which is a different path again.
 `rem-computed` exists to reach the standard emit path -- the reg-locals
 fast path fires only when both operands are plain locals -- so both
 paths of the builtin are covered rather than only the quick one.

Section: The two remainders

  rem : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rem (a) (b) = int-rem a b

  eucl : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  eucl (a) (b) = int-mod a b

  rem-computed : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rem-computed (a) (b) = int-rem (a + 0) (b + 0)

Section: The identity that names the builtin

  ident-ok : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ident-ok (a) (b) = a == (a / b) * b + int-rem a b

  ident-count : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ident-count (acc) (a) (b) = if ident-ok a b then acc + 1 else acc

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Report

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("7 rem 3 = " & show (rem 7 3))
   print-line-uni ("-7 rem 3 = " & show (rem (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni ("7 rem -3 = " & show (rem 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("-7 rem -3 = " & show (rem (0 - 7) (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("7 mod 3 = " & show (eucl 7 3))
   print-line-uni ("-7 mod 3 = " & show (eucl (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni ("7 mod -3 = " & show (eucl 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("-7 mod -3 = " & show (eucl (0 - 7) (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("-1 rem 8 = " & show (rem (0 - 1) 8))
   print-line-uni ("-8 rem 8 = " & show (rem (0 - 8) 8))
   print-line-uni ("-9 rem 8 = " & show (rem (0 - 9) 8))
   print-line-uni ("-1 mod 8 = " & show (eucl (0 - 1) 8))
   print-line-uni ("0 rem 5 = " & show (rem 0 5))
   print-line-uni ("computed -7 rem 3 = " & show (rem-computed (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni ("computed 7 rem -3 = " & show (rem-computed 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("identity -7 3 = " & yn (ident-ok (0 - 7) 3))
   print-line-uni ("identity 7 -3 = " & yn (ident-ok 7 (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("identity -7 -3 = " & yn (ident-ok (0 - 7) (0 - 3)))
   print-line-uni ("identity -1 8 = " & yn (ident-ok (0 - 1) 8))
   print-line-uni ("identity 13 4 = " & yn (ident-ok 13 4))
   print-line-uni ("identities held = " & show (ident-count (ident-count (ident-count (ident-count (ident-count 0 (0 - 7) 3) 7 (0 - 3)) (0 - 7) (0 - 3)) (0 - 1) 8) 13 4))
  end
